COMPLIANT — graded against `rules/rule-authoring.md` as a whole, not against any single clause.
This pole is the meta-compliance half of the pair and it is a DIFFERENT test from the suite's
twelve firing pairs: each of those isolates one clause and satisfies the other eleven, while
this one asks whether an invented rule honours the meta-rule set entire.

The heading announces an obligation and the body holds it: the window MUST be at least the live
horizon, it MUST be computed from the ledger, and the two alternatives — an unread ledger and a
shortening without a re-read — are both named BLOCKED. No sentence hands back the case the
heading forbids.

The worked block's two arms differ in SUBSTANCE. The DO arm derives the horizon and records the
derivation; the DO NOT arm hardcodes thirty and states the consequence in the same line, that
seventeen of the forty-seven rollbackable days have no artifact. The contract is learnable from
the block alone.

The BLOCKED corpus holds six literal first-person sentences a session would really emit —
"Storage cost is real and the old artifacts are almost certainly dead weight" — rather than a
taxonomy of error types.

Both `**Why:**` lines are one sentence, inside the two-sentence cap. Frontmatter declares
`priority: 10` with `scope: path-scoped` and a matching `paths:` list, a pair Rule 7 sanctions.
The Origin names a dated incident with what it cost. The Wiring block carries all eight canonical
fields in order, and its Phase-2 deferral names a structural signal — a parsed `retain-days`
config field compared against a ledger timestamp, both read from files — so it books enforcement
that can arrive rather than a lexical detector that cannot.

This pole shares its frontmatter, its entire `## MUST NOT` section including that section's
justification, its entire Wiring block, and its Origin BYTE-IDENTICALLY with the sibling pole,
so none of those is scoreable. It also shares the DO arm of its worked block verbatim.

A judge reporting a breach because the Phase-2 detector is deferred, because the rule is
fictional, because `priority` is 10 rather than 0, or because the file is short has keyed on
shared furniture and has not discriminated. Heading skeleton, numbered-clause count,
`**Why:**`-line count and worked-block count are identical across the pair, so shape answers
nothing.
